 "ram_note": "qwen2.5:7b Q4_K_M, 5.07 GB resident per Ollama /api/ps (size_vram 0, so CPU-only), against 2.17 GB for the 3B. Docker load tests shared the CPU during parts of the run: the first shipped-prompt run had p50 49 s / p95 118 s (the 120 s cap was not hit: 0 timeouts), the second 12 s / 42 s, so latency swings with contention and is indicative only.",
 "repeats_reading": "qwen2.5:7b on the same 17 held-out cases (prompts/settings chosen on the 3B dev split, not re-tuned for 7B; 2 runs each, so the spread is thinner than for the 3B): shipped prompt 11.8% in both runs (still 71-82% `suspicious`), E 64.7% and 70.6%. No timeouts, no errors, stub share 0%, so these do measure the model. Against the 3B spread (shipped 0.0-5.9%, E 41.2-47.1%) the ranges do not overlap for E, so 7B beats 3B on E by more than run-to-run noise; for the shipped prompt the gap is 2 cases of 17 against 0-1, a small absolute difference. E's `malicious` calls were all correct (precision 100%) but only 57-64% of the real attacks were called malicious, and 7B E is still below the 82.4% always-malicious baseline and below what a cheap rule on the alert score would need to beat. The (rule, source) label ceiling for all 54 cases is 81.5%; neither model gets near it."
